[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router2
diagnosis_text:
The issue is not with routing/RIP; it is with router2’s packet filter. FRR/RIP is healthy and advertising all far networks. Evidence: router0 and router1 FIBs show RIP-learned routes to 1.0.0.0/8 and 128.168.0.0/16 via router2 (e.g., on router1: R>* 1.0.0.0/8 via 20.20.0.2, and on router0: R>* 1.0.0.0/8 via 10.10.0.2). On router2, “show ip rip” lists learned routes from 20.20.0.1 and connected networks 1.0.0.0/8 and 128.168.0.0/16, confirming ripd is running and exchanging routes.

The failure mechanism matches a Layer-3 ACL drop at router2: traceroutes from IT/CS hosts reach router2 (hops 192.168.1.1 → 10.10.0.2 → 20.20.0.2) and then stop. Router2’s netfilter shows explicit forwards are being dropped by source subnet:
- iptables: -A FORWARD -s 192.168.1.0/24 -j DROP; -A FORWARD -s 192.168.2.0/24 -j DROP
- nftables: table ip filter chain FORWARD drops the same sources, with counters incrementing.
This blocks only IT (192.168.1.0/24) and CS (192.168.2.0/24) traffic to any network behind router2, while Other (192.168.3.0/24) and Principle (192.168.4.0/24) are unaffected—exactly the user report.

Localization: router2, netfilter FORWARD chain affecting transit from eth2 (20.20.0.2) toward eth0/eth1. Root cause: unintended firewall rules added during maintenance that drop 192.168.1.0/24 and 192.168.2.0/24.

Corrective fix: remove the FORWARD drop rules in both iptables and nftables (or flush the filter chains), then persist the clean policy. No action needed on ripd; it is up and functional.
=== END ===